ny  villages  throughout our whole territory and  almost every river system  in our territory  was utilized by our people but when the  newcomers arrived they put us on these  little plots of land  known as indian reserves  colonialism brought catastrophe here  there is a premeditated plan to sever  our relationship from the land  so that they could get access to the  resources in the bounty of the land and  the sea  at contact the heltsuk numbered upwards  of 20 000 people across 50 villages  then came harrowing waves of smallpox  and influenza  their people were brought to the brink  at one point fewer than 200 remained  the horrors and injustices of the  colonial era continued residential  schools the loss of the big house the  banning of the potlatch  their culture and language forced  underground the cornerstone of this our  society is the potlatch system and that  was our system of governance and how  resources and wealth was redistributed  through the community the idea to  separate us from our place was  devastating it's created all kinds of  social issues  health issues  mental health issues and even impacting  our spirits  [Music]  many of the old villages have faded into  the landscape but there are signs of  renewal  in bella bella a landmark of the past  has been restored to life  this is the big house the heart of the  community  it's the home of the potlatch and a  place of gathering that was destroyed in  the colonial era  there's a lot of intergenerational pain  and there's a lot of intergenerational  brilliance  over a decade in a making these  form-line house posts were painstakingly  designed carved and painted and tell of  the deep origins and resilience of a  people  chief frank's daughter gottawas is  withheld took climate action she says  the strength of the future draws from  the well of the past  we're working really hard to  connect all those dots back good morning  those innate values she says empower her  people to fight climate change w